#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891e9004-961b-539a-ab93-0752347fd077" :question/math-academy-id "e-541" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d573eb2d-82ab-5413-8bcb-b9b2336a830d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296381} {:answer/id #uuid "62ebf476-68aa-5bd7-bff4-c6c6a4b750c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=5{x}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296382} {:answer/id #uuid "98f15021-418c-5d7a-81fc-5a641671a6c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296383} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef8da88-22a3-5a8a-8a1d-60b6223fcbb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296384} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f49ccd-93ef-5294-bc32-626551ecf864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d573eb2d-82ab-5413-8bcb-b9b2336a830d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296381} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e931c97-953c-5bd7-9dfc-1e428ad98edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5938f28-6b10-54af-bd46-b5bd83651a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-14313" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}$  that satisfies $y(1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere   $C=3{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.  \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & ={1}^{3}+C \\\\ 8-1 & =C \\\\ C & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}={x}^{3}+7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38cd088b-8c79-5386-a1ca-0b764fdb511c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2x^3-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38cd088b-8c79-5386-a1ca-0b764fdb511c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2x^3-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296388} :answer-field/id #uuid "375c5fbb-2e58-5428-aa32-345b96779d2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66d0e457-c6f5-5966-8c94-b8d2ddac2421" :question/math-academy-id "q-350957" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y}=6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}$ that satisfies $y(1)=0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y} & =6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}-{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(6{x}^{2}-1){e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =(6{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =2{x}^{3}-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {1}^{3}-1+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e20c6bba-6022-5997-aa9a-58790672ebd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+1)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296391} {:answer/id #uuid "36f8ef4a-d466-5e27-9632-1ba0c811f4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-1)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36f8ef4a-d466-5e27-9632-1ba0c811f4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-1)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296392} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e36a3b-29b9-5717-92d3-0819ec1a5c2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a0c1a6f-49f7-5e1b-8197-0268b6fe21e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-355564" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(-\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4$ that satisfies $y(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x-1}{xy}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x}{2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2+\\frac{2}{2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =(2+\\frac{2}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2+\\frac{2}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K(2x-1){e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K(2(1)-1){e}^{2(1)} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K{e}^{2} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=(2x-1){e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b309eca-74f7-5b49-bd78-db38996b5ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296395} {:answer/id #uuid "2e81344c-5810-55eb-b37a-cd7e5b7b78d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e81344c-5810-55eb-b37a-cd7e5b7b78d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296396} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4d5d54c-ef9d-5325-a682-474a74c91206" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3a0c150-d1a0-56a4-b28e-6e1eb94bcefb" :question/math-academy-id "q-355579" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}$  that satisfies $y(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =2\\sqrt{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C}\\, \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75d13c89-12e5-5819-b190-541428dbca14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2-e^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296399} {:answer/id #uuid "32a1d111-a5d3-52c3-9b17-a247a2636772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2+e^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75d13c89-12e5-5819-b190-541428dbca14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2-e^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296399} :answer-field/id #uuid "800aa3ce-4273-5f23-8ff3-c955acf012d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe04fef3-6150-519b-9e35-cabeb0ad595f" :question/math-academy-id "q-355582" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =0 \\\\ C-1 & =1 \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{-x}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4641fa43-5ee8-5a98-a998-8306f96eab45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290369} {:answer/id #uuid "d67abf21-4258-5d25-a6bf-4116fb2dc38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290370} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc01640-f9b4-5f31-9081-7b2cadc0f221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290371} {:answer/id #uuid "d3937128-3f8b-53e0-91dc-8c5fd9f9b870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290372} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb4d639-0119-517a-9dff-02201f38f90f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d67abf21-4258-5d25-a6bf-4116fb2dc38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290370} :answer-field/id #uuid "a51a5481-c1f2-538c-bf44-ea05615e2d8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eab8a97a-f952-5f16-9442-1a3ecf58a82a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50220" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{1+C} \\\\ 1+C & =1 \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5004f8ab-bcfd-5b95-9df0-163643659ee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296403} {:answer/id #uuid "6469cc98-0d3e-505a-a4bd-ad22baaed83a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296404} {:answer/id #uuid "da9e9b0c-b4cd-5345-b2e9-dce18a822acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y=\\sqrt{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296405} {:answer/id #uuid "0938ef89-ec65-56dd-9c08-fbac94ee08a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y=\\sqrt{2x+7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296406} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc6e12d-cbc7-5f30-9434-b017a69c5a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccc6e12d-cbc7-5f30-9434-b017a69c5a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296407} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc0999a9-e763-5a12-a30b-184d1afbb9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bedb039-83dc-5e1b-b856-f023ba4d6c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-50566" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x$  that satisfies  $y(-3)=1.$  Assume that $y>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{C}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $y>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(-3)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+C} \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{9+C} \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x$ that satisfies the condition $y(-3)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7a3bc39-a922-5f64-bfd1-2a19006f0aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x+{e}^{-3x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296410} {:answer/id #uuid "9873e707-2fd2-5249-a343-87db775dbe1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296411} {:answer/id #uuid "96361bee-48b6-5d73-b545-1b1ca709bc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+{e}^{-3x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296412} {:answer/id #uuid "e083fe15-44ca-5ac8-bc24-f2c898194dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296413} {:answer/id #uuid "db8ab467-ee18-56ba-90df-79102eaa92a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-3{e}^{-x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9873e707-2fd2-5249-a343-87db775dbe1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296411} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a28f488-2cc3-5e9b-baa4-b221f207a20a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b83618f6-c01a-5048-a3a3-6b622350db67" :question/math-academy-id "q-79371" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x{e}^{x}=3$ that satisfies $y(0)=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3+2x{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{e}^{-x}+2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{e}^{-x}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 3{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-3{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(0)=-3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =-3 \\\\ -3{e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}+C & =-3 \\\\ C-3 & =-3 \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of ${e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x{e}^{x}=3$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-3$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-3{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d02a14-92d3-5033-a057-9bd1ad6769e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3{e}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292431} {:answer/id #uuid "c9561dd3-9d79-5ea4-b343-de193f957c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{e}^{{x}^{2}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292432} {:answer/id #uuid "727c76bb-14cb-5a53-83e6-bad976b7c23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292433} {:answer/id #uuid "05fe0008-1fb0-5e91-98a0-60bf44c51f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2{e}^{{x}^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292434} {:answer/id #uuid "024eeaad-af55-5751-84c8-a041b7337058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}={e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9561dd3-9d79-5ea4-b343-de193f957c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}=3{e}^{{x}^{2}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292432} :answer-field/id #uuid "cddc7849-107c-5824-b5d6-93695259dd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02d23995-f689-5881-b3ec-6224edc19266" :question/math-academy-id "q-79425" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x{y}^{3}=4x$ that satisfies $y(0)=1.$  Assume that $y>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x{y}^{3} & =4x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x(2+{y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2+{y}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2+{y}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2+{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={x}^{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral on the left-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2+{y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nThen  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2+{y}^{3}}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{2+{y}^{3}}(3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{y}^{3})+{C}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $y>0.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{y}^{3})+{C}_{2} & ={x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{y}^{3}) & ={x}^{2}+{C}_{1}-{C}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{y}^{3})} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{C}_{1}-{C}_{2}} \\\\ 2+{y}^{3} & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}-{C}_{2}}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(0)=1$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3} & =K{e}^{{0}^{2}}-2 \\\\ 1 & =K-2 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{e}^{{x}^{2}}-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee7b41c4-6d99-5e49-9242-d1e09126a719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296417} {:answer/id #uuid "2130de2e-cc23-57a6-8e34-fa5e0fec0fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296418} {:answer/id #uuid "701fb091-d6ed-52b2-9dbc-34a7d8122808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296419} {:answer/id #uuid "614d8a46-8296-5657-af99-99d91e61bc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296420} {:answer/id #uuid "99498ada-4103-5335-a8b5-12a34cfd23a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "701fb091-d6ed-52b2-9dbc-34a7d8122808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296419} :answer-field/id #uuid "d32f41e7-63e4-5787-8002-217ab0c5c16d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eaf4214-5b2e-5a79-b5f4-dc081a8c4d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8308" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y$  that satisfies $y(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x+C}\\, \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{x+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=1$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =K{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}\\,.\n$$"}]}
